Leave the boot thread's kernel stack unregistered

The recorded stack top was a guess.  The boot thread runs on the loader's
stack, so the live frame is not the one the recorded top describes, and
CHECK_KERNEL_STACK does not ask whether the frame is inside the stack it
knows about: it accepts a stack pointer only inside the 16 KiB window just
below the recorded top and calls everything else a corrupted kernel stack.
The recorded top was placed 128 KiB above the live frame, so the live frame
could never be inside that window.  The check then fell through to the
interrupt stack test, where a boot stack pointer is always above the
interrupt stack top, and every data abort taken on the boot thread came out
as "Invalid kernel stack pointer (probable corruption)".

That is the panic run-138 died of, and it was a false one: the register dump
says the data abort was a store to address 0x380, the NULL task dereference
in vm_fault.c, and the kernel stack had nothing to do with it.  The
misleading panic is what sent the last few runs looking at the stack.

Nothing registers the boot thread's stack here, and upstream leaves
machine.kstackptr zero for it as well.  Leaving it zero is what makes the
cbz in CHECK_KERNEL_STACK (nio-check-stack-zero.patch) let the exception
through, so the fault that actually happened is decoded and reported with
its address.  When the kernel attaches a real stack to the boot thread the
normal check resumes.

The pmap_protect() call stays: the boot stack is the loader's and has to be
writable, and the kernel's own mappings do not cover it.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/stack.c b/osfmk/kern/stack.c
--- a/osfmk/kern/stack.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/stack.c
@@ -128,6 +128,40 @@
 
 	stack_addr_mask = roundup_pow2(kernel_stack_size) - 1;
 	kernel_stack_mask = ~stack_addr_mask;
+
+	/*
+	 * nio: the boot stack is the loader's and nothing registers it.  Make the
+	 * window around the live frame writable now that pmap_kernel() is the
+	 * live map.
+	 *
+	 * The stack itself deliberately stays unregistered.  CHECK_KERNEL_STACK
+	 * accepts a stack pointer only inside the 16 KiB window just below the
+	 * recorded top and calls everything else a corrupted kernel stack, so a
+	 * recorded top that does not describe the frame the boot thread really
+	 * runs on turns every data abort into a bogus "Invalid kernel stack
+	 * pointer (probable corruption)" panic.  That is what run-138 died of,
+	 * while the real fault was a NULL task dereference in vm_fault.c: the
+	 * misleading panic hid the real one for several runs.
+	 *
+	 * With the stack left unregistered the cbz in CHECK_KERNEL_STACK
+	 * (nio-check-stack-zero.patch) lets the exception be decoded, so the
+	 * fault that actually happened is reported with its address instead of
+	 * being reported as a corrupted stack.  When the kernel later attaches a
+	 * real stack to the boot thread the normal check resumes.
+	 */
+	{
+		thread_t nio_th = current_thread();
+		volatile uint64_t nio_marker = 0;
+		uint64_t nio_sp = (uint64_t)(uintptr_t)&nio_marker;
+		uint64_t nio_page = nio_sp & ~(uint64_t)0x3fffull;
+		uint64_t nio_top = nio_page + 0x20000ull;
+		if (nio_th != THREAD_NULL && nio_th->machine.kstackptr == 0) {
+			nio_th->kernel_stack = (vm_offset_t)(nio_top - kernel_stack_size);
+		}
+		pmap_protect(pmap_kernel(), (vm_offset_t)(nio_page - 0x100000ull),
+		    (vm_offset_t)(nio_top + 0x4000ull),
+		    VM_PROT_READ | VM_PROT_WRITE);
+	}
 }
 STARTUP(TUNABLES, STARTUP_RANK_MIDDLE, stack_init);
 
